  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a branched “miktoarm” polymer, containing both hydrophilic and hydrophobic arms, can organize itself in water into different nanostructures. At the top, the polymer is sketched as several chemically distinct chains joined at one central point, with labels indicating water-loving and water-avoiding segments. The lower part shows the main self-assembled outcomes of this architecture.\n\nOne outcome is a micelle, where the hydrophobic portions cluster together in the center while the hydrophilic chains extend outward into the surrounding water, forming a fuzzy outer corona. Another is a vesicle, or polymersome, drawn as a hollow spherical shell. In this case the amphiphilic molecules form a membrane-like ring, with hydrophobic segments packed within the wall and hydrophilic chains exposed on both the inner and outer surfaces.\n\nA third structure is a compartmentalized micelle, which appears to contain separate internal domains rather than one uniform core. This suggests that the different polymer arms can segregate internally while still presenting a hydrophilic exterior. Overall, the figure emphasizes that the asymmetric, multi-arm design of miktoarm polymers allows a single polymer class to produce several distinct aqueous assemblies.",
